\verb{weighted_*} forms weight each taxon by its abundance. To test them
against a null model (NRI and NTI), see \code{\link[=mpd_ses]{mpd_ses()}}.
}
\details{
For a sample with taxa \eqn{i = 1 \ldots S}, abundances \eqn{x_i}
(total \eqn{W}), and distances \eqn{d_{ij}}:
\itemize{
\item \strong{MPD:} \eqn{\sum_{i<j} d_{ij} / (S(S-1)/2)}, the mean over
pairs of distinct taxa.
\item \strong{Weighted MPD:} \eqn{\sum_{i,j} x_i x_j d_{ij} / W^2}, the
mean distance between two individuals drawn at random, with
replacement, so that pairs within a taxon count too (at distance 0).
This is picante's and phylocom's definition.
\item \strong{MNTD:} \eqn{\sum_i m_i / S}, with \eqn{m_i = \min_{j \ne i}
  d_{ij}}.
\item \strong{Weighted MNTD:} \eqn{\sum_i x_i m_i / W}.
}

The weighted forms are unchanged by scaling a sample, so counts and
proportions give the same values. "Weighted" here means weighting by
abundance; it is not the weighting of \code{\link[=weighted_unifrac]{weighted_unifrac()}}.

These are also alpha diversity metrics: \code{alpha_div(counts, 'mpd', tree = tree)}, and likewise \code{'weighted_mpd'}, \code{'mntd'} and \code{'weighted_mntd'}.

\strong{Speed and memory}

With a tree, no taxa \eqn{\times} taxa matrix is formed. Each sample's
taxa are sorted into the tree's preorder, and the tree's lowest common
ancestors of neighbours (found in constant time from a table built once)
give the part of the tree that spans them, with at most \eqn{2S - 1}
nodes; MPD and MNTD are one or two passes over it. Each sample takes
time proportional to \eqn{S \log S}, however large the tree. With
\code{taxa_dm}, each sample reads its \eqn{S^2} distances. Results are
identical for any number of \code{cpus}.

\strong{Differences from picante}

Values agree with picante 1.8.4's \code{mpd()} and \code{mntd()} (with \code{dis = cophenetic(tree)} and \code{abundance.weighted} set to match) to about
\eqn{10^{-12}}. picante computes \code{cophenetic(tree)} first, a dense
matrix of every pair of tips (3.2 GB for 20,000 tips); \code{mpd()} reads the
tree directly. phylocom's \code{comstruct} gives MNTD 0, not NA, for a sample
with one taxon.

\strong{Same names in other packages}

\code{picante::mpd()} and \code{picante::mntd()} share these names (taking \code{samp},
\code{dis} and \code{abundance.weighted}); whichever package was attached last
masks the other's. Write \code{ecodive::mpd()} to be sure.
\code{PhyloMeasures::mpd.query()} computes the unweighted MPD.
